 ('ENT_BALT_PATULAS','PER_BALT_PAGAN','Old Prussian god of the dead and the underworld (Patulas/Pikuolis/Pecols), the third of the Romuva triad, lord of night and darkness.'),
 ('ENT_BALT_MATES','PER_BALT_PAGAN','The many Latvian ''mother'' goddesses (Mātes), each presiding over a natural domain, attested in the dainas as Meža māte, Jūras māte, Zemes māte, Vēja māte, and dozens more.'),
 ('ENT_BALT_JURASMATE','PER_BALT_PAGAN','The Latvian ''Mother of the Sea'', a tutelary goddess of the waters and one of the most prominent of the Mātes in the daina folk songs.'),
 ('ENT_BALT_AITVARAS','PER_BALT_PAGAN','Lithuanian household serpent or flying drake that brings stolen wealth (grain, milk, gold) to the home it favors, appearing as a fiery flying creature.'),
 ('ENT_BALT_KAUKAS','PER_BALT_PAGAN','Lithuanian household spirit, a small chthonic being that guards the homestead and brings prosperity if honored, named alongside the barstukai.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_PANTHEON','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Collective grouping the pre-Christian Basque mythological beings reconstructed chiefly from oral tradition by José Miguel de Barandiarán in his Mitología Vasca.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_MARI','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Supreme goddess of Basque mythology, lady of the mountains who dwells in caves and governs storms, hail and the weather.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_SUGAAR','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Male serpent or dragon spirit, the consort of Mari, associated with thunder and storms; also called Sugoi or Maju.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_MAIRU','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Race of giants of Basque legend held to have built the dolmens and stone megaliths (mairubaratza).'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_BASAJAUN','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','The ''lord of the woods'', a hairy wild-man spirit credited in legend with teaching agriculture and ironwork to humans.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_BASANDERE','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','The ''lady of the woods'', female counterpart of Basajaun in Basque forest mythology.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_GAUEKO','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Genius or spirit of the night who enforces that the night belongs to him and the day to humans.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_EATE','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Storm and fire spirit announcing tempests and floods; sparsely attested in the Basque oral record.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_TARTALO','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','One-eyed man-eating giant of Basque folklore, a Cyclops-type figure paralleling the Greek Polyphemus motif.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_LAMIA','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','Female water spirits of rivers and springs, typically depicted with the feet of birds or fish.'),
 ('ENT_BSQ_URTZI','PER_BASQUE_PAGAN','DISPUTED: the word ortzi/urtzi denotes the sky/firmament and appears in weather compounds; whether it ever named a personified sky-god is contested.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_PANTHEON','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Collective grouping the deities and spirits of the pre-Christian Sámi noaidi religion of Fennoscandia.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_HORAGALLES','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Thunder god of the Sámi, his name derived from ''Thor-karl'' (Old Thor) and widely held to be a borrowing of the Norse Thor.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_BEAIVI','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Sun deity of the Sámi, source of light, fertility of reindeer and crops, and of mental health; gender varies across sources.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_MANNU','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Personification of the moon in Sámi belief; comparatively lightly attested relative to the sun.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_RADIEN_ATTJE','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','The high creator-father of the southern Sámi cosmology, a remote sky-ruler who delegates active rule.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_RADIEN_AKKA','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Female consort of Radien-attje in the southern Sámi creator-pair cosmology.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_MATTARAHKKA','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','The great mother-goddess of the Sámi who receives the soul of the child and presides over birth, mother of the three áhkká household goddesses.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_SARAHKKA','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Daughter of Máttaráhkká and the most venerated áhkká goddess, who aids women in menstruation and childbirth and dwells beneath the hearth.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_JUOKSAHKKA','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Daughter of Máttaráhkká, the ''bow-woman'' who turns a child male and protects boys and hunters.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_UKSAHKKA','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Daughter of Máttaráhkká, the ''door-woman'' who dwells by the threshold and guards the newborn child.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_LEIBOLMAI','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','The ''alder-man'' or bear-and-hunt god whose favour was sought for success in the hunt, especially the bear hunt.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_STALLO','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','Stupid, man-eating ogre of Sámi folklore who pursues humans but is regularly outwitted; a folkloric rather than cultic figure.'),
 ('ENT_SAMI_BIEGGOLMAI','PER_SAMI_TRADITIONAL','The ''wind-man'' who governs the winds and storms with his two shovels, raising and stilling the weather.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_PANTHEON','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','Collective grouping the figures of pre-Christian Magyar religion, largely a scholarly reconstruction from folklore, loanwords, and comparison since few pagan sources survive.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_ISTEN','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','Supreme sky-god of the pagan Magyars whose name was retained as the ordinary Hungarian word for ''God'' after Christianization.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_ORDOG','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','The adversary and spirit of the underworld and evil in Magyar belief, the dark counterpart to Isten, later identified with the Christian Devil.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_BOLDOGASSZONY','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','Reconstructed great mother-goddess (''Blessed Lady'') of the pagan Magyars, whose cult was assimilated to the Virgin Mary after Christianization.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_NAGYBOLDOGASSZONY','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','The ''Great Blessed Lady'', a grand or ancestral mother-goddess of reconstructed Magyar religion, later folded into Marian veneration of the Assumption.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_TURUL','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','Mythic falcon or bird of prey of the Magyar origin myth, ancestral protector of the Árpád dynasty as recorded in the medieval chronicles.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_VILAGFA','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','The ''tree of life'' or ''world-tree'' connecting the upper, middle, and lower worlds in reconstructed Magyar shamanic cosmology, the path of the táltos.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_TALTOS','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','Class of Magyar shaman-seers, born with bodily marks, who entered trance to journey between worlds, heal, and divine; modeled here as a collective class.'),
 ('ENT_HUN_HADUR','PER_HUNGARIAN_PAGAN','RECONSTRUCTED: ''War-lord'' war-god, largely a 19th-century Romantic-era literary creation rather than a securely attested pagan deity.');
